questmarks -- attribution and data provenance
=============================================


QUEST AND MISSION METADATA
--------------------------
Everything under data/ except npc_overrides.lua and fame_dialogue.lua is
GENERATED, and it comes from THREE sources.

  1. data/quest_steps.lua -- FIRST-HAND from BG Wiki.

     A local page cache (bgwiki-rest-cache/pages/, 3414 pages) was fetched from
     BG Wiki's own MediaWiki REST API by EIGHT runs sharing one cache, each with
     its own manifest:

       manifest.json           1025  the quest run, Category:Quests
       manifest-missions.json  2147  the later mission run, Category:Missions
       manifest-gapfill.json      5  titles the category walks never reached
       manifest-gapfill2.json     0  a probe that 404'd
       manifest-gapfill3.json     1  "Monstrosity (Quest)"
       manifest-gateguard.json   29  Category:Gate Guard
       manifest-gateguard2.json   1  "Argus (NPC)"
       manifest-coalition.json    6  the six Coalition hub pages

     The manifests total fewer pages than the cache holds: a page reachable from
     two runs is stored once, and prepass.py treats the DIRECTORY as the
     authority over any manifest for exactly that reason. Each run records its
     own endpoint, category enumeration and a content hash per page. Each page's
     walkthrough was then READ and compiled into a step ladder by
     tools/pipeline/. This supplies:

       the step ladder itself, and per step: kind, target NPC or monster,
       zone id, map grid reference, the item and key-item ids the step
       requires, and the ids it grants as evidence;
       plus, per quest: the job gate, the level gate, a confidence tier, and
       the start NPC where the page names one.

     Facts gathered outside those category walks live in
     bgwiki-rest-cache/supplemental/, written down as data rather than pasted
     into a script so each fact is auditable and the build needs no network.
     FOUR files, and two of them are live build inputs:

       vigil_weapons.json   Category:Vigil_Weapons, the job -> weapon mapping
                            the twenty "Unlocking a Myth" quest pages omit;
       mission_numbers.json the wiki mission NUMBER -> mission name map for the
                            Promathia, Zilart and Aht Urhgan lines, which is
                            what lets a prerequisite written as "Zilart Mission
                            17" resolve at all. emit_lua.py generates
                            tools/lib/mission_numbers.lua from it;
       gate_guards.json     the Gate Guard roster from the two gate-guard runs
                            above, read by tools/pipeline/pinned_prepass.py;
       missing_titles.txt   the titles the category walks never reached, which
                            is what the gap-fill runs were aimed at.

     This directory is COMMITTED even though the page cache beside it is not --
     it is an input, not a cache. See .gitignore.

  2. data/quest_index.lua -- FIRST-HAND, over an identity list the CLIENT owns.

     tools/build_index.lua walks the DAT id list in data/dat_names.lua and
     merges the ladders above onto it. Row identity therefore comes from the
     client's own table, which is game content rather than wiki content (see
     the dat_names paragraph below), and everything merged onto it comes from
     the page cache. NOTHING COMES FROM CHRONICLE ANY MORE: the side of that
     merge which used to hold its data is an empty table (`local wiki = {}` in
     tools/build_index.lua), and every shipped row now carries an authored
     ladder whose giver comes from that ladder or from nowhere.

     ADOULIN COALITION ASSIGNMENTS ARE READ AND NOT SHIPPED, which is a
     provenance fact as much as a scope one. SIX Coalition hub pages were
     fetched (manifest-coalition.json); their assignment tables yielded 95
     assignment records, rendered into build/rendered/ as 95 documents under six
     `<Name> Assignment Table` source_templates, and read into 95 step ladders
     that remain in data/quest_steps.lua -- so the CC BY-NC-SA obligations below
     still cover them. What changed is that tools/build_index.lua's
     EXCLUDED_AREAS keeps the rows out of the emitted index: a coalition
     assignment is a rotating offer against a shared quest-log slot, taken by
     spending Imprimaturs, and this addon's marker model describes a stable row
     with one giver.

  3. data/mission_index.lua -- on the same terms as (2).

     A second scrape was run against Category:Missions, by the same transport
     and into the same cache (tools/pipeline/fetch_wiki.py; provenance in
     bgwiki-rest-cache/manifest-missions.json, which is separate from
     manifest.json so the quest run's record stays intact). 500 pages carrying
     a `{{Mission Header}}` are now cached; 497 of them bind to a
     `mission/<area>/<id>`. Their walkthroughs were read into step ladders by
     the same stage-B pass the quests went through, and 435 of those ladders
     reach the index.

     So the mission half splits exactly like the quest half: FIRST-HAND from
     BG Wiki for the step ladder and, per step, kind, target NPC or monster,
     zone id, map grid reference, the item and key-item ids the step requires
     and the ids it grants as evidence, all over an identity list the client
     owns.

     The BINDING is not chronicle's and not a guess: a mission page states its
     own name in the `Mission Name` header field, and that name is matched
     against data/dat_names.lua, which comes from the client's own DAT files.
     The wiki's mission NUMBER is never used for it -- the two numberings do not
     agree and are not even offset by a constant.

     This is also the only part of the index that `//qm rebuild` touches in
     game.

