THREE SETS OF TERMS, AND WHICH COVERS WHAT
------------------------------------------
The operative statement is in LICENSE and LICENSE-DATA; this file is the long
form and exists to make the split auditable rather than asserted. In summary:

  CODE -- MIT.            core/, render/, tools/, questmarks.lua, the docs.
                          Full text in LICENSE.

  WIKI-DERIVED DATA --    data/quest_index.lua, data/quest_steps.lua,
  CC BY-NC-SA 3.0.        data/mission_index.lua, data/npc_overrides.lua,
                          tools/lib/mission_numbers.lua, build/authored/,
                          build/authored-quarantine/, build/rendered/, and the
                          page cache itself. Attributed field by field above.

  GAME CONTENT --         data/dat_names.lua, the dialogue fragments in
  SQUARE ENIX.            data/fame_dialogue.lua, and the `Description:` blocks
                          in build/rendered/. Not ours to license under either
                          of the above. See GAME CONTENT at the end.

WHY THE CODE IS NOT ALSO CC. A Creative Commons licence is a poor instrument
for software: it grants no patent rights and carries no software warranty
disclaimer, and CC themselves recommend against using it for code. MIT does
both jobs and is what a Windower addon's readers expect.

WHY THAT DOES NOT MAKE THE ADDON COMMERCIAL-FRIENDLY. The NC obligation is
inherited from BG Wiki. It was not chosen here and cannot be relicensed away
here. questmarks does not function without its data, so the whole cannot be
sold, put behind a paywall or a donation gate, bundled into a paid product, or
shipped inside anything monetised -- the MIT licence on the code does not lift
that. A commercial derivative would have to replace the wiki-derived data
outright and drop this file's claim to it.

WHAT IS KEPT, AND WHAT IS DISCARDED. The generated files hold only FACTS the
markers need -- ids, names, zone ids, grid references, counts, gates -- and no
prose. Concretely, tools/pipeline/emit_lua.py writes exactly:

  per quest  conf, snpc, snpcs, sz, lvl, jobs, craft, rep, prev, fame,
             fame_lvl, steps
  per step   k, npc, z, g, grp, optional, reqs_partial, reqs, reqs_alt,
             ev, ev_alt, mobs

and nothing else. The reading pass records a `note` on most steps and a longer
`notes` on most quests -- the human-readable justification for how the page was
interpreted -- and the emitter DROPS BOTH. Walkthroughs, descriptions and any
other expressive text are therefore discarded before anything reaches data/.
Keep it that way: an emitter that started shipping wiki sentences would change
what the ADDON redistributes.

  THE REPOSITORY IS A WIDER THING THAN THE ADDON, and this distinction became
  load-bearing at the first public release. build/ is not purely a working
  directory: build/authored/ and build/rendered/ ARE committed (see .gitignore
  for why -- one is the source of truth for every ladder, the other is runtime
  data for the sibling questmarks-ui addon), and both hold full wiki prose.
  So the repository does redistribute BG Wiki text, under CC BY-NC-SA 3.0, with
  per-document page id, revision id and content hash in the front matter. What
  the loaded ADDON puts on screen still carries none of it.

  BUT `steps[].note` IS READ IN PLACE, AND A PLAYER SEES IT. Dropping it at the
  emitter keeps it out of what THIS addon redistributes, and that is still the
  rule. It does not make the field private: the sibling `questmarks-ui` addon
  reads build/authored/ off disk at runtime and draws each step's note in its
  detail pane, expanded by default. So the per-step note is player-facing text
  even though nothing ships it. tools/pipeline/AUTHORING.md's "Two notes, two
  audiences" states the split, and the build gates that hold it are described
  there.

  THE WHOLE RENDERED CORPUS is 1829 files: 1022 quest pages, 95 Coalition
  assignment records, 497 bound from `{{Mission Header}}` pages, 207 from
  `{{Campaign Header}}` ones and 8 mission documents carrying neither infobox.
  Each carries the same front matter the quest half does -- page_id,
  revision_id, source_sha256, cache_file, match_rule, match_conf and the page's
  categories -- so every line traces to one cached revision of one page, plus
  `source_template` recording which of the two infoboxes it was read from.

  The `Description:` block in those files is the text of the player's own
  in-game mission log, transcribed by the wiki: SQUARE ENIX game content under
  the notice below, not CC BY-NC-SA authorship, and the same field the quest
  half has always carried. It is copied verbatim after markup removal and is
  never summarised, paraphrased or synthesised. Of the 704 Mission-Header and
  Campaign-Header documents, 697 state one; the 7 that do not get no
  `Description:` line at all rather than an invented one.

  The rest of each document -- walkthrough bullets and the sections after them
  -- IS CC BY-NC-SA wiki prose, exactly as the quest documents' walkthroughs
  already were, and it stays in build/ for the same reason they do. Nothing in
  emit_lua.py reads these files and the shipped index gains no field from them.
  The list above is still the whole of what is emitted.

data/dat_names.lua is GENERATED but is NOT wiki data. It is the id -> name table
the game itself uses, extracted from the client's own DAT files (0xD99A-0xD9BE);
chronicle is merely the tool that did the extracting. Those strings are FFXI
game content, covered by the SQUARE ENIX notice below rather than by
CC BY-NC-SA. They are used functionally -- to name a quest in a message and,
more importantly, to give core/state.lua the complete id ladder it needs to
work out which mission of a linear storyline you are on. The file is frozen:
regenerate it with `luajit tools/pipeline/dump_res.lua --names` only if the game
adds content.

WITH THANKS TO chronicle
------------------------
questmarks started as a consumer of the `chronicle` addon by jintawk
(https://github.com/jintawk/ffxi-chronicle). Its bundled BG Wiki tables are what
made the first version possible at all: they answered which quests exist, who
gives them, what they need and what they lead to, well enough to prove the idea
was worth building. Every one of those questions is now answered from this
project's own page cache and from the game client's own data, but the shape of
the answer was learned there first.

Two readings of chronicle's CODE also survive. They are acknowledgements rather
than dependencies, and they are listed once, under CODE below. Thank you.

CODE
----
core/state.lua adapts the packet 0x056 sub-type table and the bitflag decode
from chronicle's data.lua, which in turn credits the **Mastery** addon for the
original story_logs mapping. Mastery is the origin of that mapping and is
credited here for it; chronicle is where this project read it.

core/inventory.lua follows the batched multi-bag sweep pattern from
chronicle's wiki.lua.

Both are readings of its CODE, made once and cited at the point of use. Neither
opens a chronicle file; see "WITH THANKS TO chronicle" above.

core/fame.lua's text pipeline (incoming text mode 150 -> from_shift_jis ->
strip prompt and colour bytes -> split on " : ") was derived by reading the
Balloon addon (https://github.com/StarlitGhost/Balloon), which is the reference
implementation for reading rendered NPC dialogue under Windower.

Packet field definitions come from Windower's own addons/libs/packets/fields.lua.
Three readings are taken straight from it, and each is cited at the point of use
rather than remembered:

  core/keyitems.lua   the two 0x40-byte bitfields and the category number in
                      packet 0x055 (fields.lua:2784)
  core/inventory.lua  the 'itemstat' enum, of which 0x05 = Equipped
                      (fields.lua:282)
  core/kills.lua      the Param 1 / Param 2 / Message layout of packet 0x02D
                      (fields.lua:1992), plus the seven message ids in
                      Windower's res/action_messages.lua that mean a target
                      died. Combat packets are read through Windower's own
                      windower.packets.parse_action.


